# Line endings, made a property of the REPO rather than of each clone's
# `core.autocrlf`.
#
# WHY THIS IS SAFE TO ADD, measured rather than assumed: every one of the 217
# tracked text blobs is ALREADY pure LF, and `git add --renormalize .` against
# this file stages ZERO files. It changes no content. What it changes is who
# decides: without it, correctness depends on each machine's `core.autocrlf`,
# so a clone with `autocrlf=false` would commit CRLF blobs and break a
# uniformity nothing currently enforces.
#
# It does NOT dictate working-copy endings, and that is deliberate. `text=auto`
# stores LF and checks out whatever the platform wants, which is why this repo
# has CRLF and LF files side by side in the working tree while every blob is
# LF. CLAUDE.md's per-file endings ("abpage.py is CRLF, fidelity.py is LF") are
# observations of WORKING-COPY state -- an artefact of which files a tool has
# rewritten since checkout -- not of what is stored.

* text=auto

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BINARY. Every one of these is pinned by extension rather than left to
# auto-detection, because the cost of getting it wrong is not symmetric: a
# text file wrongly marked binary is a cosmetic diff problem, and a binary
# file wrongly normalised is CORRUPTION.
#
# `Commando.sng` is the sharpest case in the repo. It is the byte-exact
# reference output the whole port is verified against -- `test_commando.py`
# requires `got == ref` -- so a single translated `\n` inside it would break
# the project's only fidelity anchor, and would do it silently, because the
# failure would look like a converter regression rather than a checkout.
*.sng   binary
*.sid   binary
*.exe   binary
*.zip   binary
*.frx   binary
*.wav   binary
*.png   binary

# VB6 SOURCE IS DELIBERATELY LEFT ON `text=auto`, AND THAT WAS MEASURED.
# Marking `*.frm` / `*.vbp` / `*.vbw` as `-text` (store verbatim) looks right --
# the IDE writes CRLF -- but it stages 1349 insertions and 1349 deletions
# across three files, because their blobs are LF today and `-text` would
# replace them with CRLF. That buys nothing: `text=auto` already stores LF and
# checks out CRLF on Windows, which is what VB6 wants. The rule earned its
# place by being tried and reverted, not by being reasoned about.
